"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"][7]={"university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M7-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M7-L1 – Relational modelling and SQL for analytics<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M7 – Bachelor: Databases and data engineering (9 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/Fundamentals<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L3<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L4<\/a>;\ntables and keys.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Model tables according to their\nunit of observation and formulate aggregations with the correct join\ncardinality.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From event to relation<\/h2>\n<p>A laboratory records measurements from devices. The device table\nstores exactly one record per device, and the measurement table exactly\none per measurement event. The primary key identifies a row; a foreign\nkey references a valid device key. This avoids repeating the location\nname for every measurement. A functional dependency\n<code>geraet_id → standort<\/code> means that the same device key cannot\nsimultaneously denote different locations. If locations change over\ntime, a time-dependent relationship would instead be necessary.\nNormalisation prevents many update anomalies, but does not guarantee the\ncorrect granularity for the domain.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Joining and grouping<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001682_en.svg\"\nalt=\"A device can have several measurements; device 3 is retained by the left join.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">A device can have several measurements;\ndevice 3 is retained by the left join.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Relational granularity.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>The synthetic teaching example contains three devices, one of which\nhas no measurements. An inner join would exclude this device; a left\njoin retains it. <code>COUNT(m.id)<\/code> counts only existing\nmeasurements, whereas <code>COUNT(*)<\/code> also counts the additional\nempty row. <code>AVG<\/code> ignores SQL NULLs; an empty measurement\ngroup produces NULL, not zero seconds. Grouping is by device, not merely\nby location, which may occur more than once. The graphic shows how the\nfour measurements are associated with devices.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Executable SQL example<\/h2>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode sql\"><code class=\"sourceCode sql\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">CREATE<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">TABLE<\/span> geraet (<span class=\"kw\">id<\/span> <span class=\"dt\">INTEGER<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">PRIMARY<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">KEY<\/span>, standort TEXT <span class=\"kw\">NOT<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">NULL<\/span>);<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">CREATE<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">TABLE<\/span> messung (<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>  <span class=\"kw\">id<\/span> <span class=\"dt\">INTEGER<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">PRIMARY<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">KEY<\/span>,<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>  geraet_id <span class=\"dt\">INTEGER<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">NOT<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">NULL<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">REFERENCES<\/span> geraet(<span class=\"kw\">id<\/span>),<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>  dauer_s <span class=\"dt\">REAL<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>);<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">INSERT<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">INTO<\/span> geraet <span class=\"kw\">VALUES<\/span> (<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"st\">&#39;Nord&#39;<\/span>),(<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>,<span class=\"st\">&#39;Nord&#39;<\/span>),(<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>,<span class=\"st\">&#39;Sued&#39;<\/span>);<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">INSERT<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">INTO<\/span> messung <span class=\"kw\">VALUES<\/span> (<span class=\"dv\">11<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>),(<span class=\"dv\">12<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">8<\/span>),(<span class=\"dv\">13<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">5<\/span>),(<span class=\"dv\">14<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>,<span class=\"kw\">NULL<\/span>);<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">SELECT<\/span> g.<span class=\"kw\">id<\/span>, <span class=\"fu\">COUNT<\/span>(m.<span class=\"kw\">id<\/span>) <span class=\"kw\">AS<\/span> n_messungen,<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>       <span class=\"fu\">COUNT<\/span>(m.dauer_s) <span class=\"kw\">AS<\/span> n_gueltig, <span class=\"fu\">AVG<\/span>(m.dauer_s) <span class=\"kw\">AS<\/span> mittel_s<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-11\"><a href=\"#cb1-11\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">FROM<\/span> geraet g <span class=\"kw\">LEFT<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">JOIN<\/span> messung m <span class=\"kw\">ON<\/span> g.<span class=\"kw\">id<\/span><span class=\"op\">=<\/span>m.geraet_id<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-12\"><a href=\"#cb1-12\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">GROUP<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">BY<\/span> g.<span class=\"kw\">id<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">ORDER<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">BY<\/span> g.<span class=\"kw\">id<\/span>;<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>The results are <code>(1,2,2,6.0)<\/code>, <code>(2,2,1,5.0)<\/code>\nand <code>(3,0,0,NULL)<\/code>. When filtering by measurement time or\nquality in a left join, decide whether these filters belong in the ON\ncondition: a WHERE filter on the right-hand table can remove the added\nNULL rows. The query uses only common SQL constructs and can be checked\nlocally with SQLite.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Cardinality, exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>If two additional maintenance events for device 1 are joined to its\ntwo measurements using the device ID, four combinations result. Sums of\nmeasurement durations are doubled. Exercise: How can the number of\nmaintenance events and the sum of measurement durations be obtained\nsimultaneously for each device? Solution: First aggregate both event\ntables independently by device ID, then join the two result tables to\nthe device table. Check which metric treats missing values as zero and\nwhich treats them as unknown.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/tutorial-join.html\">PostgreSQL\n18: Joins Between Tables, § 2.6<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong>\npending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M7-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M7-L2 – Indexing and efficient analytical queries<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M7 – Bachelor: Databases and data engineering (9 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/Fundamentals<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M7-L1<\/a>;\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L5<\/a>\n(complexity).<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish selection, data\nlayout and query plans; evaluate the benefit of an index through\nmeasurement.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Logical request and physical execution<\/h2>\n<p>SQL describes the desired result, not every access step. A database\nmay scan a table sequentially, search an index or combine several access\npaths. A B-tree keeps keys ordered and supports equality and range\nsearches. The effort required to locate a key grows approximately with\nthe height of the tree; accessing the matching records adds further\ncost. An index occupies storage space and must also be maintained during\nwrites.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Selectivity and block accesses<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001596_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Model comparison: 23 block visits for a rare match versus 10,000 for a full scan; no runtime measurement.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Model comparison: 23 block visits for a\nrare match versus 10,000 for a full scan; no runtime\nmeasurement.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Index access and full scan.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>A model example: One million measurements are stored in 10,000 blocks\nof 100 rows each. A query for a rare device ID matches 20 rows. If these\nare in 20 different blocks and three index blocks are visited, this\namounts to 23 block visits compared with 10,000 for a full scan. This is\nnot a runtime prediction: caches, random accesses, index size and the\nretrieval of additional columns affect costs. With 700,000 matches, a\nsequential scan may be cheaper. Column-oriented storage also reduces the\namount of data that must be read when an analysis uses only a few\ncolumns.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Reading plans and testing hypotheses<\/h2>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode sql\"><code class=\"sourceCode sql\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">CREATE<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">INDEX<\/span> idx_geraet <span class=\"kw\">ON<\/span> messung(geraet_id);<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">SELECT<\/span> <span class=\"fu\">AVG<\/span>(dauer_s) <span class=\"kw\">FROM<\/span> messung <span class=\"kw\">WHERE<\/span> geraet_id<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>;<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>In PostgreSQL, inspect the plan for the SELECT query with\n<code>EXPLAIN<\/code> and its actual execution with\n<code>EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)<\/code>. Estimated and measured row\ncounts show where the optimiser misjudges the data profile.\n<code>ANALYZE<\/code> as an EXPLAIN option actually executes the\nstatement; this is particularly important for statements that modify\ndata. With a composite index <code>(geraet_id, zeit)<\/code>, queries for\na device within a time range are natural candidates; the suitability of\nother filters depends, among other things, on the DBMS and the plan.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>A query reads all measurements from one day, and the table is\nphysically partitioned by day. Is an index on the device sufficient?\nSolution: Not necessarily. Partition pruning can exclude irrelevant days\nif the predicate matches the partitioning scheme. An additional device\nindex helps only if the query is sufficiently selective within the\nremaining data. Compare plans, blocks read and runtimes with and without\nan index using realistic data volumes; small teaching tables do not\nprovide a production benchmark.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/indexes-intro.html\">PostgreSQL\n18: Introduction to Indexes, § 11.1<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong>\npending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/using-explain.html\">PostgreSQL\n18: Using EXPLAIN, § 14.1<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong>\npending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M7-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M7-L3 – Data integration, ETL and ELT<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M7 – Bachelor: Databases and data engineering (9 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/Fundamentals<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L4<\/a>\nto <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L8&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L8<\/a>;\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M5-L1<\/a>;\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M7-L1<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Design repeatable data\nintegration with domain-specific transformation and error isolation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Integration rather than simply copying files<\/h2>\n<p>Two measurement systems report durations in seconds and milliseconds\nrespectively; one uses local time, the other UTC. Before combining them,\nthe units, time reference, keys, missing-value encodings and provenance\nmust be known. A technical data type of REAL does not establish a common\nunit. The target model includes <code>event_id<\/code>,\n<code>zeit_utc<\/code>, <code>dauer_s<\/code>, <code>quelle<\/code> and the\nprocessing revision. Event IDs must be unique across sources, for\nexample through a composite source key.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Processing order<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001567_en.svg\"\nalt=\"ETL transforms before loading; ELT first loads into the target area and transforms there.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">ETL transforms before loading; ELT first\nloads into the target area and transforms there.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: ETL and ELT compared.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>ETL extracts data, transforms it in an upstream processing step and\nloads the result into the target dataset. ELT first loads the extracted\ndata into suitable target or staging storage, then uses the computing\ncapacity there. In practice, hybrid approaches exist. With ELT, raw data\nmust not automatically become visible to all analysts. With ETL, the\nreproducibility of the transformation must be ensured, for example\nthrough permitted input snapshots and code versions. Erroneous rows are\nisolated with a reason rather than silently deleted.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Repeatable teaching example<\/h2>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>raw <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> [(<span class=\"st\">&#39;A&#39;<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&#39;10&#39;<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">2500<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&#39;ms&#39;<\/span>), (<span class=\"st\">&#39;B&#39;<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&#39;3&#39;<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&#39;s&#39;<\/span>),<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>       (<span class=\"st\">&#39;A&#39;<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&#39;11&#39;<\/span>, <span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&#39;ms&#39;<\/span>), (<span class=\"st\">&#39;A&#39;<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&#39;10&#39;<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">2500<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&#39;ms&#39;<\/span>)]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>accepted, rejected <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> {}, []<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> source, event_id, value, unit <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> raw:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    key <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> (source, event_id)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> unit <span class=\"kw\">not<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> {<span class=\"st\">&#39;s&#39;<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&#39;ms&#39;<\/span>} <span class=\"kw\">or<\/span> value <span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        rejected.append((key, <span class=\"st\">&#39;ungueltige Dauer oder Einheit&#39;<\/span>))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">continue<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    seconds <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> value <span class=\"op\">/<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">1000<\/span> <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> unit <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"st\">&#39;ms&#39;<\/span> <span class=\"cf\">else<\/span> value<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> key <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> accepted <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> accepted[key] <span class=\"op\">!=<\/span> seconds:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-11\"><a href=\"#cb1-11\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">raise<\/span> <span class=\"pp\">ValueError<\/span>(<span class=\"st\">&#39;widerspruechliche Ereignis-ID&#39;<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-12\"><a href=\"#cb1-12\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    accepted[key] <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> seconds<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-13\"><a href=\"#cb1-13\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> accepted <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> {(<span class=\"st\">&#39;A&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"st\">&#39;10&#39;<\/span>): <span class=\"fl\">2.5<\/span>, (<span class=\"st\">&#39;B&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"st\">&#39;3&#39;<\/span>): <span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>}<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-14\"><a href=\"#cb1-14\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">len<\/span>(rejected) <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>The duplicate row does not change the final dataset. The example\ndeliberately omits timestamps and persistent transactions: a real\npipeline must manage version selection, the restart position and atomic\ningestion between the source and the target store. An upsert with a\nstable ID prevents duplicate entries only if all associated changes are\nhandled consistently.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>After a network failure, a batch that has already been transferred is\nreplayed. How can duplicate revenue entries be prevented? Solution:\nAssign a stable batch or event ID, store the ingestion state atomically\nwith the target change, and acknowledge the source only after successful\npersistence. In the event of partial failures, the restart must apply\nthe same domain rules. Simply adding the amount again to the existing\ntotal is not idempotent.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://docs.delta.io/kernel/rust/writing/idempotent_writes\">Delta\nKernel: Idempotent writes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong>\npending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/de-de/fabric/data-warehouse/dimensional-modeling-overview\">Microsoft\nLearn: Dimensional modelling, star schema design<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> –\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M7-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M7-L4 – Data warehouses, lakes and lakehouses<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M7 – Bachelor: Databases and data engineering (9 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/Fundamentals<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M7-L1<\/a>\nto <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M7-L3<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Compare storage architectures in\nterms of data form, transactions, access and analytical objectives.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Three architectural principles<\/h2>\n<p>A data warehouse provides integrated data structured for analysis,\noften with relational semantics and defined metrics. A data lake stores\nheterogeneous data, such as audio files, logs and column-oriented files,\nin object or file storage. A lakehouse adds a managed table layer to a\nlake, for example consistent table snapshots, schema rules and\ntransaction rules. These terms denote architectural principles, not\nmutually exclusive product classes. A collection of files alone does not\nprovide a reliable shared table view.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Facts and dimensions<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001713_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Central measurement facts with device key, date key and measurement duration, linked to two dimensions.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Central measurement facts with device\nkey, date key and measurement duration, linked to two\ndimensions.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Star schema for laboratory analytics.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>A star schema for laboratory operations contains one row per\nmeasurement event in the fact table, with <code>geraet_key<\/code>,\n<code>datum_key<\/code> and <code>dauer_s<\/code>. Device and date are\nseparately described dimensions. Fact granularity must be established\nbefore the metrics are selected. A device may historically have had a\ndifferent location; a versioned dimension with validity intervals allows\nhistorical measurements to be associated with the location that was\nvalid at the time. The key then denotes the dimension version, not\nmerely the physical device.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Metrics from subsets<\/h2>\n<p>A location provides 2 valid measurements with a mean of 5 s on Monday\nand 8 with a mean of 9 s on Tuesday. The weekly mean across all ten\nmeasurements is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>9<\/mn><\/mrow><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>8.2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar x=\\frac{2\\cdot5+8\\cdot9}{2+8}=8.2\\,\\mathrm{s}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The unweighted mean of the daily means (7 s) answers a different\nquestion. For aggregated measurements, therefore, store the sum and\ncount if subsequent roll-ups are required. Transaction logs in a table\nformat can version data and metadata changes together; the isolation and\ncompatibility guarantees depend on the specific format, connector and\noperating environment.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>A team trains models on video recordings and also analyses daily\ndevice counts. Must it copy all videos into relational tables? Solution:\nNo. Videos can reside in a controlled object store, while metadata,\naccess information and analyses are organised in a warehouse or managed\nlakehouse tables. Reproducible models require unambiguous data versions,\nnot merely the latest file path.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/de-de/fabric/data-warehouse/dimensional-modeling-overview\">Microsoft\nLearn: Dimensional modelling, star schema design<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> –\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://docs.delta.io/\">Delta Lake: Documentation\noverview<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M7-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M7-L5 – Batch and stream processing<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M7 – Bachelor: Databases and data engineering (9 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/Fundamentals<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M4-L1<\/a>;\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M7-L3<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Combine event time, processing\ntime, windows, watermarks and corrections with clear justification.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Bounded and ongoing inputs<\/h2>\n<p>A batch processes a bounded input dataset, such as the complete file\nfor one day. A stream consists of a sequence of events that is generally\nnot bounded in advance. A system may process the stream in small batches\nor pass on events individually; the domain semantics are not determined\nsolely by the technical processing cadence. Continuous processing\nrequires state management, recovery and a rule for when results are\nemitted.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Two clocks and a window<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001716_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Three events belong to the first time window; one arrives late, after the initial output.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Three events belong to the first time\nwindow; one arrives late, after the initial output.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Event time and arrival time.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>We count measurements in the time windows [0,60) and [60,120) s from\nthe start of observation, using event time. Let the pairs (event time,\narrival time) be (10,12), (50,55), (65,67) and (30,90). An initial\nresult for the first window is emitted at arrival time 70, after the\nwatermark has passed the window boundary: two events. Despite arriving\nat 90, the fourth event belongs to the first minute. An allowed lateness\nthat still includes this event permits a correction to three; the second\nwindow contains one so far. Watermarks are progress estimates or\nsystem-specific guarantees, not universally exact statements about\nremaining events.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Emission, replay and correction<\/h2>\n<p>Triggers determine when provisional and subsequent results are\nemitted. A dashboard can maintain traceable versions using\n<code>(fenster_start, revision)<\/code> or replace an existing window\nrow. If, instead, first two and then three are processed as additive new\ncontributions, the incorrect value five results. Deduplication by event\nID and persistent checkpoints limit the effects of replay. End-to-end\nexactly-once processing requires compatible source, state and sink\nsemantics; the designation of a streaming engine alone is insufficient.\nFor events arriving beyond the permitted lateness, an explicit strategy\nfor discarding, reprocessing or handling errors is necessary.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>The window size is 60 s and the event timestamp is exactly 60 s.\nWhich window contains the event? Solution: [60,120), because the\nright-hand boundary is excluded. If it arrives only after the allowed\nlateness has expired, the documented late-data rule determines its\nprocessing, not the arithmetic rule for window assignment.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://beam.apache.org/documentation/basics/\">Apache Beam:\nBasics of the Beam model, Windowing / Watermarks / Trigger<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> –\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://docs.delta.io/kernel/rust/writing/idempotent_writes\">Delta\nKernel: Idempotent writes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong>\npending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M7-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M7-L6 – Partitioning, distributed processing and scaling<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M7 – Bachelor: Databases and data engineering (9 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/Fundamentals<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L5<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L8&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L8<\/a>;\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M7-L2<\/a>\nand <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M7-L5<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Analyse distributed aggregation,\ncommunication costs and data imbalance.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Compute locally, exchange selectively<\/h2>\n<p>Partitions divide a dataset into units that can be processed\nseparately. Range partitioning by date can facilitate selective time\nqueries; hash partitioning by key attempts to distribute keys across\nseveral partitions. In a grouped analysis, contributions for a given key\nmust be brought together. This exchange of data between computing nodes\nis called a shuffle and can place demands on the network, memory and\ndisks. Storage partitions and processing partitions are not necessarily\nidentical.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Reduction before the shuffle<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001630_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Three local sum and count pairs are combined into (72,9); overall mean 8.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Three local sum and count pairs are\ncombined into (72,9); overall mean 8.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Local aggregation before the shuffle.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>Three processing units provide the following (sum, count) pairs for\nthe same key: (18,3), (24,4), (30,2). Instead of transmitting nine\nindividual values, three pairs can be transmitted and added\ncomponent-wise. The overall result is (72,9), with a mean of 8. The\nthree local means of 6, 6 and 15 would give an unweighted mean of 9 and\nthus an incorrect overall estimate. Associative and commutative\ncombinations are particularly suitable for local pre-aggregation;\nfloating-point rounding may cause small numerical differences when the\norder changes.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of speed-up<\/h2>\n<p>For an idealised parallel fraction\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nequally fast computing units, Amdahl’s model is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S(k)=\\frac{1}{(1-p)+p/k}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.9<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=0.9<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k=8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe result is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>4.71<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S\\approx4.71<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nnot eight. Communication and coordination costs are omitted from the\nmodel. In addition, skew produces unequal partition sizes: with four\nnodes working simultaneously at the same processing rate and shares of\n70%, 10%, 10%, 10%, the largest partition determines the duration of\nthis idealised stage. The speed-up relative to one node would be at most\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>0.7<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.43<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/0.7\\approx1.43<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nprovided that no other costs arise.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>In a join, the dimension contains only 1,000 rows, while the event\ntable contains several million. What alternative to exchanging both\ntables is available? Solution: The small dimension can be replicated to\nthe computing nodes (broadcast join), provided it fits into available\nmemory and the plan supports the desired join semantics. Check actual\nsizes, skew and network costs before forcing replication.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-performance-tuning.html\">Apache\nSpark: SQL Performance Tuning, join strategies / partitioning / skew<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>\n– <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M7-L7":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L7\">M7-L7 – Data contracts, lineage and quality assurance<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M7 – Bachelor: Databases and data engineering (9 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/Fundamentals<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L6<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L7<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M5-L1<\/a>;\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M7-L3<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Define data quality as a\nverifiable contract and reconstruct results along their lineage.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Contracts are domain agreements<\/h2>\n<p>A data contract defines what providers and users of a dataset may\nexpect from one another. This includes the schema, units, keys, time\nreference, null rules, permissible values, freshness limits and\nresponsibilities. An additional optional column may be compatible;\nswitching from seconds to milliseconds without changing the column name\nis, by contrast, a semantic break, even if the type and test values look\nthe same. Changes require versioning rules and clear migration\ndeadlines.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From contract to measurable test objective<\/h2>\n<p>A daily batch contains 100 events. Of these, 96 have a non-missing\nduration, and two of those 96 are negative. Completeness relative to all\nevents: 96/100 = 96%. Value validity relative to available values: 94/96\n≈ 97.92%. The proportion of usable events relative to all events is\n94/100 = 94%. Different denominators must remain visible; otherwise\napparent quality is not comparable. A contract may require at least 98%\ncompleteness and no negative durations, for example. Both rules would be\nviolated here. The affected events can be isolated and a release step\nblocked.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Lineage and run logs<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001544_en.svg\"\nalt=\"100 input events, 4 missing and 2 invalid values; 94 usable events, release subject to rules.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">100 input events, 4 missing and 2 invalid\nvalues; 94 usable events, release subject to rules.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Quality checks and data lineage.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>Data lineage connects input datasets, processing steps and outputs. A\nrun may document the snapshot <code>raw@17<\/code>, transformation code\n<code>v3<\/code>, an input count of 100, a quarantine count of 6 and the\npublished snapshot <code>clean@8<\/code>, for example. In the graphic,\nthe published state is only a hypothetical result if the release rule\npermits partial publication; otherwise the run stops before publication.\nOpenLineage is an example of a standardised event model comprising\ndatasets, jobs and specific runs. Lineage is no guarantee that the\ncomputational logic is correct. Tests of schemas, keys, domain\ninvariants, distribution shifts and regressions are also useful.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>A model’s accuracy falls from 90% to 70% after a data import. Which\nlineage information helps with diagnosis? Solution: The input snapshot,\ntime window, feature/transformation version, quality reports, training\nstate and evaluation data version allow controlled comparisons. An\nobserved distribution shift is initially a warning signal, not yet proof\nof a faulty pipeline.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://openlineage.io/\">OpenLineage: Standard API for\nlineage events<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M7-L8":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L8\">M7-L8 – Data protection and access control in data pipelines<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M7 – Bachelor: Databases and data engineering (9 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/Fundamentals<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L5<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M7-L3<\/a>\nand <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M7-L7<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Translate data protection\nprinciples into specific technical safeguards and verifiable access\nseparation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Protection begins before storage<\/h2>\n<p>For personal data, the purpose, legal basis and necessary attributes\nmust be clarified. Article 5 of the General Data Protection Regulation\n(GDPR) requires, among other things, purpose limitation, data\nminimisation, storage limitation and protection of integrity and\nconfidentiality; Articles 25 and 32 concern data protection by design\nand security of processing. A teaching example is not legal advice: the\napplicable legal basis and retention period depend on the actual\nprocessing activity. An app that only analyses daily device counts may\nnot need a user’s email address for that purpose.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Separating roles, keys and data<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001665_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Raw dataset, transformation service and approved analytics table with separate roles and a separately protected mapping.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Raw dataset, transformation service and\napproved analytics table with separate roles and a separately protected\nmapping.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Access roles in a data pipeline.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>One possible pipeline holds raw personal data in a restricted area. A\ntransformation service produces pseudonymised events for analysis and\nremoves unnecessary fields. The mapping table linking pseudonyms to\nindividuals must be protected separately, for example through separate\npermissions and keys. Pseudonymised data may still be personal data;\neven aggregated data may allow inferences in small groups or when linked\nwith additional knowledge. Encryption in transit and at rest reduces\nindividual risks, but replaces neither access control nor lawful\nprocessing.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>An example permissions matrix<\/h2>\n<p>The ingestion service may write new raw events, but not read\narbitrary raw data; the transformation service may read the required\ninput dataset and write the minimised table; the analytics role receives\nonly read permissions for approved tables and no access to the mapping.\nAdministrator rights are separated and logged. Also define deletion and\nretention rules for replicas, backups, caches and logs. A successful\naccess test with an authorised role is not sufficient: negative tests\nmust demonstrate that unauthorised accesses fail.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>An analyst receives only a table of hashed email addresses. Is it\nanonymous? Solution: Not automatically. Email addresses can be\nreconstructed using candidate lists or linked to other datasets; a hash\nwithout additional safeguards is particularly vulnerable to this.\nIdentifiability must be assessed in context. Avoid unnecessary\nidentifiers and check purpose, access, key management and deletion.\nAnonymisation is a stronger requirement than simply replacing names.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj/eng\">Regulation (EU)\n2016/679, in particular Arts. 4, 5, 6, 25, 32<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> –\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M7-L9":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L9\">M7-L9 – Streaming algorithms: sketches, reservoirs and approximate counting<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M7 – Bachelor: Databases and data engineering (9 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective specialisation<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M4-L2<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M4-L7<\/a>;\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M7-L5<\/a>\nand <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M7-L6<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Explain reservoir sampling,\nCount-Min Sketch and distinct counting as different memory\ntrade-offs.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A sample without a known total length<\/h2>\n<p>A stream contains\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nelements, with the total length unknown at the start. Reservoir sampling\n(Algorithm R) stores the first\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nelements. At element number\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i&gt;k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nan integer from 1 to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>i<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis drawn uniformly; if it is in the range 1 to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe element replaces the corresponding reservoir position. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N\\ge k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\neach element has a final selection probability of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k/N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAn already stored element is retained at the next step with probability\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1-1/i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>⋅<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k/(i-1)\\cdot(i-1)/i=k/i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe new element is included with probability\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k/i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis establishes the individual inclusion probability inductively. For a\nuniform sample of k distinct positions, the stronger induction also\ngives equal probabilities for all subsets of size k.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Frequencies with Count-Min Sketch<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001537_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Two hash rows, A has frequency five; in row one B collides with A, but not in row two. The minimum of the marked counters is five.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two hash rows, A has frequency five; in\nrow one B collides with A, but not in row two. The minimum of the marked\ncounters is five.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Count-Min Sketch with collisions.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>A Count-Min Sketch consists of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>d<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrows of counters with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>w<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncells each and suitable independent hash functions. For every\nnon-negative update of key\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe counter at its hash position is incremented in each row. The\nestimate is the minimum of the associated counters. Without deletions,\nor with non-negative updates, and without counter overflow,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover><mi>f<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≥<\/mo><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat f_x\\ge f_x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nholds because collisions only add further mass. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>w<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⌈<\/mo><mi>e<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>ε<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⌉<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w=\\lceil e/\\varepsilon\\rceil<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⌈<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mi>δ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⌉<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d=\\lceil\\ln(1/\\delta)\\rceil<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwith suitable independent hash functions the additive bound\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover><mi>f<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≤<\/mo><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>ε<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat f_x\\le f_x+\\varepsilon N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nholds with probability at least\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>δ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1-\\delta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor a fixed chosen key; here\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndenotes the total insertion mass. With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.01<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon=0.01<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.05<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\delta=0.05<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthis gives 272 columns and 3 rows, or 816 counters. To justify this: For\none row, the expected collision mass under pairwise independent uniform\nhashing is at most\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>w<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N/w<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nMarkov’s inequality bounds the probability of exceeding\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nby\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>w<\/mi><mi>ε<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/(w\\varepsilon)\\le1/e<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAll\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>d<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nindependent rows therefore fail simultaneously with probability at most\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>δ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e^{-d}\\le\\delta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe minimum may be correct despite collisions: the graphic shows two\ncounters of 7 and 5 for A, with a true frequency of 5.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A different task: counting distinct keys<\/h2>\n<p>The number of distinct event IDs differs from the frequency of a\nparticular key. HyperLogLog uses hash values and registers that store\ninformation about rare bit patterns; cardinality is estimated from many\nregisters. For large cardinalities in the typical asymptotic regime, the\nrelative standard error of classical HLL is approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.04<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mi>m<\/mi><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.04/\\sqrt{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>m<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nregisters; with 4,096 registers, this is about 1.625%. This is not a\nhard error bound; hash quality, correction methods and the cardinality\nrange affect accuracy. Reservoirs, by contrast, are actual samples of\nevents that can be used, for example, to estimate measurement\ndistributions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Computational experiment and exercise<\/h2>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> random<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>rng <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> random.Random(<span class=\"dv\">42<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> reservoir(stream, k):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    sample <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> []<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> i, value <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">enumerate<\/span>(stream, <span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> i <span class=\"op\">&lt;=<\/span> k:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            sample.append(value)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">else<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            j <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> rng.randrange(i)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> j <span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span> k:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-11\"><a href=\"#cb1-11\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>                sample[j] <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> value<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-12\"><a href=\"#cb1-12\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> sample<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-13\"><a href=\"#cb1-13\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>hits <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> reservoir(<span class=\"bu\">range<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">20<\/span>), <span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>) <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> _ <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">range<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">10000<\/span>))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-14\"><a href=\"#cb1-14\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(hits <span class=\"op\">/<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">10000<\/span>)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>The expected value is 0.2; random deviation is normal. Exercise:\nWhich structure would you choose for the number of distinct user\npseudonyms in a very large number of events? Solution: A cardinality\nestimator (e.g. HLL) if estimation error is tolerable; a Count-Min\nSketch instead answers frequency questions, and a reservoir alone does\nnot count distinct users reliably. Data protection and permitted storage\nalso apply to sketch states.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.cs.ox.ac.uk/people/graham.cormode/pubs/html/CormodeMuthukrishnan04CMJalg.html\">Cormode\nand Muthukrishnan (2005): An improved data stream summary: the count-min\nsketch and its applications, Journal of Algorithms 55(1), 58–75<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> –\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1145/3147.3165\">Vitter (1985): Random\nsampling with a reservoir, ACM TOMS 11(1), 37–57<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> –\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://dmtcs.episciences.org/en/articles/3545\">Flajolet,\nFusy, Gandouet, Meunier (2007): HyperLogLog: the analysis of a\nnear-optimal cardinality estimation algorithm<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> –\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
